---
const rows = [
  ["Noun", "Names a person, place, thing or idea", "The ", "crowd", " cheered."],
  ["Verb", "Shows an action, occurrence or state", "The crowd ", "cheered", "."],
  ["Adjective", "Describes a noun or pronoun", "The ", "excited", " crowd cheered."],
  ["Adverb", "Adds information about a verb, adjective or adverb", "The crowd cheered ", "loudly", "."],
  ["Pronoun", "Refers to a noun or noun phrase", "", "They", " cheered."],
  ["Preposition", "Shows a relationship, often in a prepositional phrase", "They cheered ", "after the match", "."],
  ["Conjunction", "Joins words, phrases or clauses", "They cheered ", "because", " they had won."],
  ["Determiner", "Specifies a noun: which one, whose, or how many", "", "The", " crowd cheered."],
  ["Interjection", "Expresses a reaction or feeling", "", "Hurray!", " They had won."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Parts of Speech - What are they?</h1>
  <p>
    Every word has a job in a sentence. That job is its <strong>part of speech</strong>. Knowing the job
    helps you understand sentences and choose or correct words in grammar questions. A word’s job can
    change with its use, so always look at the whole sentence.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Quick reference</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <colgroup>
        <col class="concept" />
        <col class="practise" />
        <col class="example" />
      </colgroup>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Part of speech</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Main job</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([part, job, before, word, after]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{part}</th>
              <td>{job}</td>
              <td>
                {before}
                <strong>{word}</strong>
                {after}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    Articles (<strong>a, an, the</strong>) are a type of determiner. Some school materials count them
    separately. The important point is to identify the word’s job in its sentence.
  </p>
</div>
